{
  "id": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
  "featureId": "ebb9ceb0-ea12-4374-af6e-12aa4256bcc3",
  "number": 82,
  "shortId": "FH5BA",
  "priority": 19,
  "slug": "plugin-11-layer-content-from-a-viewnode-paint-and-input-through-the-layer-stack",
  "title": "plugin-11: Layer content from a ViewNode + paint and input through the layer stack",
  "discussedAt": "",
  "contextReady": false,
  "contextReadyReason": "",
  "summary": "The layer registry ships with native content only. Its own comments say the ViewNode path (plugins) and paint/input wiring land later — this is that work.",
  "description": "Transferred from docs/surface-compositor.md §9 on 2026-07-26, before that file was deleted. §§1–8 of it are IMPLEMENTED; §9 was marked DRAFT and is now built. **Updated 2026-08-12** — the original plugin-11 work has landed and the phase now carries the exposé that was built on it.\n\n═══ WHAT SHIPPED — the layer stack (the phase's original scope) ═══\n`heca/src/chrome/layers.rs` (1150 lines): `LayerBand` (semantic z bands, no magic numbers), `LayerKind::{Persistent, OnDemand}`, `ShowLayer`/`HideLayer`/`ToggleLayer`, and — the part §9 said would land later — **`LayerContent::{Native(Box<dyn Component>), View(ViewNode)}`** :208, realized through the one bridge (`chrome/realize.rs`). Layers paint in stack order and input routes to the active or modal layer (T336). `add_layer` returns a stable `LayerId` a plugin can hold.\n\nThe migration order from §9 is complete:\n1. ~~Introduce the stack, register the existing surfaces, hints read it~~ — DONE.\n2. ~~Convert the confirm dialog to a layer with hintable buttons~~ — DONE (T320), as an `OverlayHost::open_modal` with a ViewNode body and actions, not a bespoke modal.\n3. ~~`ShowLayer` on-demand layers → the first native exposé~~ — DONE (T327's first half; its **exit sequencing**, fade over shrink, is the open remainder and waits on a layer's lifetime following the whole exit).\n4. ~~The ViewNode content path → plugins add layers~~ — DONE (T339).\n\n⚠️ **The KeyHint half of §9 is obsolete as written.** It described `collect_hint_targets` finding subtrees carrying `.hint_target(...)`. That API is **deleted**: `HintTargetRegistry`, `HintTargets`, `HintTargetId`, `Base::hint_target` and `named_press` were removed by F004/P084/T399 under ⭐⭐ RULE ZERO, because a plugin could construct none of them. A pick is now **one line on the widget** — `KeyHint::new(w).on_peek(…)` — collected out of the laid-out tree by `heca_grid_ui::collect_peeks` and run by `fire_peek`. Nothing is registered, so nothing has to be un-registered when a tree rebuilds. Do not reintroduce any of the deleted names.\n\n═══ WHAT THE PHASE IS NOW — the exposé ═══\nThe first real layer is the **exposé** (`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs`, 2028 lines): a bird's-eye of every workspace, one row each, with a `CardGrid` cursor, surface-scoped delete keys (T416) and the open/close zoom effect. It is where the layer stack is proven, and where the remaining tasks are:\n\n- **T417** — the layer stack becomes the surface TREE: z is a path, not a band.\n- **T418** — the map's hover behaviour. The reveal now follows the **keyboard**, never the pointer (`CardGrid::reveal_state()` + `Row::reveal_when`), which Antonio confirmed. Two reported symptoms — the animation \"is strange\", a card is hard to click — are **still unconfirmed**, and may be consequences of the reveal loop that is now fixed. Check before changing anything.\n- **T419** — the fit: the zoom is computed from the session's extents, not configured. Shipped in `4eb3136` as `fit_zoom`, and **superseded by T420**, which deletes it: under shares there is no zoom to compute.\n- **T420** — split `map()` into components: `ExposeGrid` / `WorkspaceRow` / `ColumnCard` / `PaneCard`, **beside the surface** in `heca/src/chrome/expose/`, sized by **shares** (`Length::Pct` of one shared denominator, `grow` weights) with **no pixel scale anywhere**. This is the phase's centre of gravity — see its own description and the phase handoff.\n- **T421** — the map is laggy with many panes (26 across 3 workspaces), worst on open/close. Prime suspect, **unmeasured**: `layout_layers` (`chrome/mod.rs`) recomputes a full `LayoutEngine::compute` over every visible layer each frame with no dirty check. Second: the frosted backdrop's full-screen blur pass per animation frame. **Measure first.**\n\n═══ THE DECISIONS THAT GOVERN THE REMAINING WORK (Antonio, 2026-08-12) ═══\n- **Components, Flutter/SwiftUI style**: *\"reason in terms of Components that encapsulate their internal logic and are reusable. Like in React but write code Flutter/SwiftUI style.\"* A surface is components, never one function that draws a picture.\n- **They live beside their surface** (`heca/src/chrome/expose/`) and move up to `heca/src/components/` when a second surface needs them.\n- **Every size is a share; nothing is a pixel.** The exposé's `map()` computed a zoom and emitted `Length::Px(col.width * zoom)` — which is taking over the layout engine's job, and then owning every term (window, overlay margin, panel padding, card gaps, row count, row heights, scroll centring). Seven attempts, six wrong, each missing a different term.\n- **The zoom EFFECT stays.** `effects::Zoom` + `LayerRegistry::set_zoom` (with `PaintCx::with_scale` underneath) is the open/close animation and is already generic for any layer including a plugin's. Only `overview_zoom` / `overview_scale` — the *fit* scale — goes.\n- **Measure before fixing.** Six wrong guesses in one day; instrumenting named the cause in one round, every time.\n\n═══ THE INVARIANTS THAT MUST SURVIVE (§6 of the deleted file) ═══\n- NO hardcoded z or levels anywhere. z is tree position; occluders come from real layout geometry.\n- Buttons NEVER declare a layer. A button inherits its layer from the surface it is mounted in.\n- The active context is moved ONLY by context surfaces (exposé, modal) — never by zoom, scroll or provider selection.\n- Occlusion is decided by draw order plus geometry, not by the coarse level.\n- Overlays and modals are children of their opener, and exclusive when modal.\n- ONE rule for visibility. If you are special-casing a surface's hints, the surface is mis-modelled — fix the model, do not add a filter.\n\n═══ BUILD ON, DO NOT DUPLICATE ═══\n`OverlayHost` is NOT a second stack — it is built ON this one. `open_modal` realizes the ViewNode body plus its action buttons into a native tree, pushes it as a Modal-band layer, and resolves the returned result when a button's intent fires. Its overlays ARE layers here.\n\n═══ VERIFICATION ═══\nLayering, paint order, hint visibility and the map are all **visible** behaviour. Green tests are not verification: Antonio drives every visual check, and green tests found none of the exposé's seven failures.",
  "notes": "",
