    {
      "id": "7028cfd0-d674-4d51-ac5c-97213108ca6c",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 419,
      "shortId": "2XX4S",
      "priority": 8,
      "shortName": "the-expos-fits-everything-on-s",
      "title": "The exposé fits everything on screen — the zoom is computed, not configured",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "**Antonio, 2026-08-12:** *\"is it right that some cards are shown outside the view? shouldn't we zoom out the whole area and the pane increases so that we can see them all at sight?\"* — and, on the shared-zoom question: *\"Ok\"*.\n\n# The defect\n\nAn exposé shows everything at once; that is what the name means. Today the map uses a **fixed** zoom (`[settings] overview_scale`, default `0.5`, read at `heca/src/chrome/expose.rs:363` as `geometry.overview_scale`) and scrolls whatever does not fit. So on a session wider than two screens the cards run off the edge and the surface stops being a map of the session and becomes a window onto part of one.\n\n# What to build — one shared zoom, fit to the view, clamped at both ends\n\n**One zoom for the whole map**, not one per row: Antonio chose this. It keeps the workspaces comparable in size so the map reads as one picture, where a per-row fit makes a two-pane workspace's cards huge beside a ten-pane one.\n\nThe zoom becomes computed, with `overview_scale` demoted from *the* zoom to a **maximum**:\n\n```\nfit   = min(fit_horizontal, fit_vertical)\nzoom  = min(fit, overview_scale).max(floor)\n```\n\n- **`fit_horizontal`** — the widest row must fit. Everything needed is already on the model\n  (`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs:80` `ExposeWorkspace`): `strip_width` is the sum of the columns\n  (already resolved by the layout, never recomputed), and `viewport` is `(x, width)` — the screen.\n  ⚠️ **Floats can stick out past `strip_width`**: `ExposeFloating` carries its own `x`/`w`, so the\n  row's true extent is `max(strip_width, max(float.x + float.w))`.\n- **`fit_vertical`** — N rows plus the gaps between them must fit. The gap is\n  `viewport_h * overview_gap * zoom` (niri's tenth-of-a-screen, `expose.rs:444`), so the height is\n  `zoom * viewport_h * (N + (N-1) * overview_gap)` and the fit falls out as\n  `1 / (N + (N-1) * overview_gap)` — independent of the pixel height, which is the tell that it is\n  the right form.\n- **`floor`** — a card must stay legible. A *scale* floor does not guarantee that, because columns\n  differ in width; the honest floor is a **minimum card width in logical pixels**, so\n  `floor = min_card_width / narrowest_column_width`. Under it the map overflows and scrolls again,\n  which is the only honest answer for thirty panes.\n\n⚠️ **`min` then `max`, never `clamp`** — `f64::clamp` panics when `min > max`, and a user with a\nsmall `overview_scale` and a large minimum card width is exactly that case. Legibility wins there;\nsay so in the doc comment.\n\n# The setting\n\n`overview_min_card_width` beside the other three in `LayoutOptions`\n(`heca-core/src/layout/types.rs:183`, where `overview_scale` / `overview_zoom_from` / `overview_gap`\nalready live), fed from `heca-config/src/settings.rs` like its neighbours.\n\n**AGENTS § 2**: a new `SettingsConfig` field that is not written into `config.default.toml` is a bug\n— the default then lives only in code, invisible to the user. Add it there with a comment, and to\n`README.md` beside the other overview settings. `overview_scale`'s own documentation has to change\ntoo: it is now a **maximum**, not the zoom.\n\n# What falls out for free\n\nIf nothing overflows, nothing scrolls — so the autocentring problem T418 addresses stops existing\nin the common case rather than being suppressed. Do **not** treat that as a reason to revert T418:\na session past the floor still scrolls, and the hover loop would still be there waiting.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- **One workspace, one pane** — `fit` is large, `overview_scale` caps it, so the map does not blow\n  a single pane up to fill the screen.\n- **An empty workspace** — `strip_width` falls back to the viewport (`expose.rs:94`), so a row of\n  nothing is still a screen-shaped row and the fit is not divided by zero.\n- **A workspace with no columns but floats** — the extent must still come out positive.\n- The **zoom animation** on open (`set_zoom`, `overview_zoom_from`) multiplies whatever this\n  resolves to; it is a separate transform and needs no change.\n- `heca/src/chrome/expose.rs:995` and `:1156` assert card sizes against\n  `LayoutOptions::default().overview_scale` — they will need the computed zoom instead, and that is\n  the point: a test that hardcodes the old constant would pass while the map fits nothing.\n\n# Verification (Antonio drives)\n\n- Several workspaces, one wide with many columns: **every card is on screen**, and the widest row\n  just fits.\n- A small session: cards do not balloon — `overview_scale` still caps it.\n- A session past the floor: cards stay legible and the map scrolls, rather than shrinking to\n  slivers.\n- Reload (`prefix+Shift+r`) after changing `overview_min_card_width` in `config.toml` takes effect.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\n**Done — the goal, by a different mechanism (2026-08-13).**\n\nThe task's goal was \"the exposé fits everything on screen — the zoom is computed, not configured\". It shipped that way in `4eb3136` as `fit_zoom` + `overflows`, driven by Antonio with a geometry trace after four wrong corrections.\n\n**T420 then deleted both.** Under shares there is no zoom to compute and no fit that can fail: a column is a percentage of one shared denominator, a pane and a row are `grow` weights, and taffy resolves them against whatever room the window gives. The map fits **by construction**, at every window size — which is the goal, more strongly than a computed zoom could hold it.\n\nWhat replaced the arithmetic: `ExposeGrid`'s `the_whole_map_never_exceeds_the_box_it_is_given` (4 sessions × 4 window sizes, rows of unequal height), plus `a_stack_never_exceeds_the_column_it_is_given`. The seven fit tests went with `fit_zoom`.\n\n`overview_min_card_width` — the floor this task added — is deleted end to end. `overview_zoom` / `overview_scale` survives but **no longer reaches the exposé**; see the follow-up filed about the dormant core overview mode that is now its only reader.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "2026-08-12T22:21:58.873Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-08-12T22:21:58.873Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-12T08:47:42.555Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T22:21:58.873Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "e139107d-dc27-4e5d-84b7-0d8adc9393d3",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 420,
      "shortId": "GDRND",
      "priority": 9,
      "shortName": "the-expos-fits-against-the-wro",
      "title": "Split the exposé into components — ExposeGrid / WorkspaceRow / ColumnCard / PaneCard, sized by shares",
      "status": "in-progress",
      "description": "**Antonio, 2026-08-12:** *\"I want you to reason in terms of Components that encapsulate their internal logic and are reusable. Like in React but write code Flutter/SwiftUI style.\"* — *\"we need to split the expose in components. ExposeGrid, WorkspaceRow, ColumnCard, PaneCard and align them using Flex or Grid. Each component encapsulates its internal business logic, accepts properties, events and callbacks.\"*\n\n# BUILT 2026-08-13 — uncommitted on `feat/palette-modes`. ⏳ AWAITING ANTONIO'S DRIVE.\n\nWorkspace tests green (517 + the rest), clippy 0 warnings with `--all-targets --all-features`. **Not verified visually** — that is the last step and it is his.\n\n## What the surface is now\n\n`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs` (2028 lines, one ~400-line `map()`) → `heca/src/chrome/expose/`:\n\n| file | lines | owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `mod.rs` | 557 | host wiring: `register()`, `callbacks()`, `map()` (**40 lines**), `share_v()` |\n| `model.rs` | 282 | the session reduced to data (unchanged behaviour) |\n| `pane_card.rs` | 356 | one pane: name, cursor/focus, delete letters, activate |\n| `column_card.rs` | 145 | the panes' **vertical** shares |\n| `workspace_row.rs` | 336 | the columns' **horizontal** shares + the floats' rects |\n| `expose_grid.rs` | 322 | the rows' shares of the map + the cursor |\n| `testing.rs` | 119 | the shared fixtures (`#[cfg(test)]`) |\n\nEach takes properties as struct fields (Flutter-style named parameters), events/callbacks through one `ExposeCallbacks` group, and **never `&AppState`**. `PaneCard`/`ColumnCard` return their concrete widget so the parent can still size them; a boxed component has no builders left.\n\n## The sizing, as built\n\n- **column width** = `Pct(col.width / extent)` inside a strip of `Pct(extent / widest)`, `widest` = the widest extent in the **map**. Two levels, one denominator each; multiplied out it is `col.width / widest`, so every row is at the same scale.\n- **pane height** = `share_v` (`grow` + zero base + shrink — CSS `flex: 1 1 0`).\n- **row height** = `share_v(row, viewport_h + gap)`.\n- **the gap** = a share too: each row's allotment includes it and the strip takes `Pct(screen_h / (screen_h + gap))`, centred, so half sits above and half below and two rows meet as one full gap. `overview_gap` keeps its meaning (a tenth of a screen) at every window size.\n- **air between columns** = the ColumnCard's own `pad_x`, **not** the row's `gap` — a gap is added *outside* the width shares, so the widest row would come out at 100% + its gaps and overflow by exactly that. Padding is inside the border box. Vertically a gap is safe (`grow` divides what is left after gaps), so the panes use one.\n- The only `Length::Px` left in the whole surface is the `Px(0.0)` flex base inside `share_v`.\n\n## ⚠️ The decided float mechanism had a hole — asked, and a new library capability landed\n\nPercentage margins **cannot** place a float. Measured, not assumed: a `Pct` margin resolves against the parent's **width on both axes** (CSS's rule; taffy implements it faithfully), so `margin_top(Pct(y / viewport_h))` silently produces the wrong number on any row that is not square. Guard: `heca-grid-ui/src/layout.rs::a_percentage_margin_resolves_against_the_parents_width_on_both_axes`.\n\nPut to Antonio with three options; he chose **a fractional-rect builder in `heca-grid-ui`**:\n\n- `style::Placement { left, top, width, height }` — four `Length`s, `Serialize`/`Deserialize`;\n- `Layout::placement: Option<Placement>` → taffy `position: Absolute` + per-axis `inset`, overriding `width`/`height`;\n- **`LayoutExt::at_rect(left, top, width, height)`** — one line, on every widget, and out of the flow so a float draws *over* the strip instead of pushing it along. The `Grid`-cell stacking trick the old map used is gone.\n- The **declarative half works and is proven**, not assumed (AGENTS' \"adding serde is not the same as being able to author it\"): `heca-view-realize::a_description_can_place_a_node_at_a_fractional_rect` authors it as a `PropValue::Map`.\n- Docs: rustdoc on the builder + `docs/widgets.md` → \"Placing a widget at an app-chosen rect\" now carries an `at_rect` subsection for both audiences.\n\n## Deleted\n\n`fit_zoom`, `overflows`, `share`, both `ScrollRegion`s in the map, `SCROLL_EASE`, the three debug traces (`expose fit:` / `expose laid out:` / `center_pad:`), the `#![allow(dead_code)]` seam allow, and `overview_min_card_width` end to end (`LayoutOptions` + `SettingsConfig` + `config.default.toml` + `startup.rs`). `SURFACE` is now actually used by `register` instead of the literal it had drifted from.\n\n**Kept:** the zoom *effect* (`set_zoom` + `overview_zoom_from`), the model, `CardGrid`, the `[[keys.surface]]` letters, the intents, the confirm, `pane_nav_key`.\n\n## Tests — 25 in the surface, one per component plus the one that matters\n\n- `PaneCard` — shows its name; its cell's signal **is** its focus signal; the letters act on the pane/column/workspace it sits in; an unbound letter dispatches nothing; a real press+release click chooses it.\n- `ColumnCard` — panes in proportion to their real heights; **the stack never exceeds its column**, at 1/2/3/12 panes.\n- `WorkspaceRow` — columns in proportion against the map's denominator; a short row is centred; **a float lands at its own fraction on BOTH axes**; a float displaces nothing; a float past the strip's end widens the extent.\n- `ExposeGrid` — ⚠️ **`the_whole_map_never_exceeds_the_box_it_is_given`**: 4 sessions × 4 window sizes, including rows of unequal height. This is the assertion that would have caught every one of the seven failures. Plus rows in proportion, the gap, one card focused at a time, the cursor walking onto a float.\n- `mod.rs` — the shipped letters reach the cards; the defaults bind them; the cursor is handed on before a delete; activate and dismiss reach the grid through the overlay; no workspace label is drawn and the map is all in the overlay layer; the assembled map stays inside the window at three sizes.\n\nDropped with the arithmetic they tested: the seven fit tests (`the_map_zooms_out_until_the_widest_row_fits`, `a_small_session_is_capped_rather_than_magnified`, `there_is_no_minimum_card_width_by_default`, `cards_stop_shrinking_at_the_minimum_width`, `a_floor_above_the_ceiling_does_not_panic`, `the_rows_fit_stacked_with_their_gaps`, `rows_of_different_heights_are_summed_not_multiplied`) and the two centring tests.\n\n# Verification — Antonio drives, and this is the whole of what is left\n\nThree workspaces, ~26 panes: everything on screen at once, margins that look deliberate, the wheel moves nothing, resizing re-fits live, clicking a card the pointer rests on activates first time, and the open/close zoom still plays.\n\n⚠️ **Expect one deliberate change in the picture**: the map now fills **both** axes independently, so a row's aspect is no longer the screen's. That is what \"the margins disappear under shares\" means — whichever axis used to bind left slack on the other. If it reads wrong, the fix is a decision about aspect, not about arithmetic.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
